A||N||11||Call||Automatic or Manual? Also need some opinion on another question.||||5||
Z||000000||Call||01-16-2000||05:20 PM||||I have a chance to purchase a 1985 Fiero here. The ad says: 1985 PONTIAC Fiero, V6. New A/C and tires, good condition, for $1700, does this sound like a good car to anyone? I havent had the chance to look at it yet or find out more about it. When I call I am going find out whether it is a manual or automatic which brings me to my next question, I originally planned on buying an automatic but if this one is an manual I may go ahead and purchase it because for me to purchase a Feiero most of the time I would have to go out of state to get one and since this one is here its more convenient and in good condition. So what are all of your opinions on an automatic or a manual transmission? Thanks!||||reg||5
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||01-16-2000||05:56 PM||||I guess it's a matter of personal taste really. The manuals are a bit faster than the automatics, but my personal preference goes to the automatics. Also a lot of problems discussed here are clutch related so I guess an automatic has less problems.<P>$1700 for a 85GT sounds fair enough. Be aware though that it's not the fastback model.||||reg||1
Z||000002||88formula||01-16-2000||08:10 PM||||I've driven both and I'll be the first to tell you that the manual is way more fun.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Shiner||01-16-2000||10:14 PM||||What kind of question is that?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>I would go out of your way to get a manual trans car if you have to.  They are MUCH more fun to drive, and if you like to spin tires, get a stick.   The automatics do seem to have less problems, and if that is a big issue to you, go with an auto, but on those long trips you were talking about, the gas mileage will suffer (the TH125 is a 3 speed)||||reg||6
Z||000004||ChadMan||01-17-2000||10:39 AM||||It's probably a good car.  The price is reasonable if it looks and runs good - dicker down for exceptionally high miles, worn interior or faded paint.  Is it a GT or an SE?  Some people don't like the looks of the 85 SE, because it looks just like the base coupe.<P>The manual is quite a bit quicker than the automatic, but sooner or later, you'll be putting a clutch in it (or at the very least, new clutch hydraulic components).  If the clutch takes a lot of effort to depress or doesn't seem to fully disengage, be aware that you'll soon be spending $800-$1000 on clutch work!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Greg Piet||01-17-2000||01:38 PM||||I have driven both, and it depends on personal preference.  If you want to do really silly things, like wear away your tires real quick, go with a stick.  If your driving in constant stop and go id say go with an auto.  I drive in Las Vegas with a stick, which isnt as bad as New York City, but it doesnt bother me in the least(untill i have to replace the clutch).||||reg||1
Z||000006||mrfixit58||01-17-2000||02:35 PM||||Auto vs Manual...HMMMM... age old debate. Regarding speed, unless your planning on autocrossing the car, I don't think the stick is faster than the auto. I mean, you may feel it in the seat of your pants when you drop the clutch but as for raw speed, maybe a half a car length in the 1/4 mile. (flame suit "ON").<P>Regarding gas milage on long trips, no difference unless you buy a five speed with overdrive. The final gear ratio in sticks and automatics is one-to-one. When the autos torque converter locks up there is no slippage. Where the gas mileage thing come into play is for city driving. And, this assumes your not hot rodding the stick.<P>Like someone said before, if you drive a lot in stop-and-go traffic, an automatic is more comfortable, if you like to impress your frends with smoking tire burnouts... buy a V8. Chirping tires never impress me, they only make me laugh.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||HotFieroGT||01-18-2000||11:06 PM||||I have two Fiero GT's, both 88's and one is an auto and the other is a stick.  I bought the auto years ago and always wanted to drive a stick.  Finally found a 5-speed car a few months ago, the auto is going up for sale as soon as possible!||||reg||6
Z||000008||Patrick||01-18-2000||11:17 PM||||<BR>No contest, get a manual tranny. (And this is from someone who's had more than their share of Fiero clutch problems.)<P>Here is as good a place as any to ask this question - Did the '85 GT come with a 5-speed or just a 4-speed?||||reg||14
Z||000009||Standard||01-18-2000||11:54 PM||||85 GT came with a muncie 4spd.  the first v6 with a 5 spd was the 86.5 GT.  GM didn't use the Isuzu 5spd that came on the 4 cyl in 85 because they believed it couldn't handle the torque that the V6 produced.  It can, but I don't know how long it would hold up.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Patrick||01-19-2000||12:07 AM||||<BR>Thanks Standard.||||reg||14
Z||000011||2M4 Dale||01-19-2000||12:12 AM||||Does it say Pontiac Fiero on it ? It's fun to drive...NUFF SAID||||reg||6
